{
  "_doc": "Window colors. Section palette (Shared/WindowColor.swift): one case, expect = WindowColor.palette in order; new windows are dealt these. Section normalized: input {raw} -> '#RRGGBB' upper case, or null unless raw (trimmed of whitespace and newlines, one optional leading '#') is exactly 6 hex digits. Section hex: input {red, green, blue}, each a number or the string 'nan' / 'inf' / '-inf' -> '#RRGGBB' with each component clamped to 0...1, times 255, rounded half away from zero; a non-finite component is 0. Section first_unused (WindowManager.firstUnusedPaletteColor, Mac): input {avoiding:[hex]} -> the first palette color not in avoiding, or null. Section colors_to_avoid (WindowManager.colorsToAvoid, Mac): input {all:[hex], shown:[hex]} -> sorted list: all while some palette color is outside all, else shown. There is no pure window-id -> color function on the Mac: colors are dealt in turn (first unused, then a rotation) and remembered per id. Expected values are written by the Swift test (QUIP_WRITE_VECTORS=1), never by hand. Some hex inputs (e.g. 0.5/255) sit next to a rounding boundary: a reader must parse JSON floats exactly, to the nearest double (Rust: serde_json with the float_roundtrip feature), or it may round to a different byte.",
